  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A warm front on Thursday will bring the next \nchance for widespread rain. \n\nBy Thursday, high pressure moves offshore with southerly return\nflow behind a warm front will bring dew points into the low \n70s. Showers and possibly some thunderstorms will start moving \ninto western areas before daybreak and continue through the day.\nLatest hi res guidance is now in range of Thursday afternoon and\nare showing some better organization of convection.\n\nAs for the hazards, there is a SLIGHT (2 out of 4) risk for \nexcessive rainfall. Precipitable water values increase to \nperhaps as high as 150% of normal for this time of year. \nThe hi res CAMs are suggesting that the heaviest precipitation \nwill be generally along the I-95 corridor and points northwest.\nGenerally around 0.5 to 1.0 inch of rain is expected across the\nregion, but neighborhood probabilities are showing the risk for localized\nQPF values in excess of 2-3 inches. Widespread flash flooding\nstill seems unlikely, but the localized threat has increased. \n\nFor severe hazards, latest CAM guidance has come in more bullish\nfor convective development in the afternoon. HREF mean SBCAPE\nvalues are around 1000-2000 J/kg with ensemble maximums around\n3000 J/kg. While deep layer shear is lacking a bit (around 25\nkt), hodographs are showing veering winds aloft to support a\nmostly multicell threat. Furthermore, steep low-level lapse\nrates will support rapid updraft development. Will need to\ncontinue monitoring hi res trends to see if this threat holds.\nSPC has expanded the area of a slight risk to now include the\nPoconos, Lehigh Valley, and Berks County.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front will cross the area Friday.\n\nThe timing of this cold front will be critical in the risk for \nthunderstorm hazards. There are differences between models in \nboth the timing, and if this will be a single cold front, or an \ninitial cold front with a reinforcing shot of cold air advection\ncoming in behind it. Saturday and Sunday appear to be dry across\nmost of the area with the exception of southern areas Saturday\nwhere low probabilities of showers and storms will exist.",
